VBF: vectorial Boolean function analysis
========================================

VBF is a Julia workspace for analysing vectorial Boolean functions, with a focus
on APN (almost perfect nonlinear) functions and the linear / affine / EA / CCZ
equivalence hierarchy. It reimplements, in native Julia, the algorithmic content
of the sboxU cryptanalysis stack and several satellite projects (the Xanadu
apn-ccz-graph-framework, heggebakk's ea-equivalence, cbe90's self_equivalent_apn
and quadratic-APN search, the APN-Repository, NIchanska's QAM code, and
telegrin's 3-to-1 equivalence). It does not depend on any of those repositories
at run time or build time.

The workspace has two packages:

  VBF (packages/VBF)
      Representations, F2 linear algebra, spectra, EA/CCZ invariants, the
      equivalence predicates, and the construction/search routines.

  VBFCatalog (packages/VBFCatalog)
      The data layer (uses VBF). The curated SQLite catalog, multi-source
      reconciliation, the bibliography, data importers, and bulk corpora.


REPOSITORY LAYOUT
-----------------

The git repository root is a Julia [workspace] with no package of its own:

  packages/VBF/         the analysis package
  packages/VBFCatalog/  the data/catalog package (depends on VBF)
  papers/               standalone paper-reproduction scripts
  docs/                 Documenter.jl source for the API reference site


QUICK START
-----------

Requires Julia >= 1.12 (the [workspace] feature, introduced in 1.12, is the
floor). From the repository root:

  julia -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.activate("packages/VBF"); Pkg.instantiate()'
  julia -e 'using VBF'         # the algorithms
  julia -e 'using VBFCatalog'  # the algorithms plus the catalog

Run the test suite (ReTestItems, parallel):

  julia -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.activate("packages/VBF"); Pkg.test()'


DOCUMENTATION
-------------

The full API reference is generated from the source docstrings (every public
name carries its mathematics and references) and published at

  https://zambonin.github.io/VBF.jl/

The VBF source is grouped by concept, one directory per chapter under
packages/VBF/src:

  representations/  SBox, F2 linear/affine maps, Spectrum, ANF, finite fields,
                    univariate polynomials
  linalg/           F2 (GF(2)) bit-packed linear algebra and subspace search
  spectra/          DDT, Walsh/LAT, boomerang, algebraic degree, BPT anomaly
  invariants/       ortho-derivative/integral, sigma multiplicities, mugshot,
                    design 2-ranks, bent and image structure
  equivalence/      the linear / affine / EA / CCZ hierarchy and 3-to-1 tests
  constructions/    switching neighbours, quadratic-APN search, trims/extensions,
                    QAM, known families, sporadic instances, Taniguchi
  random/, io/      seeded generators and Markdown/LaTeX rendering

To build the docs locally:

  julia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'
  julia --project=docs docs/make.jl

The HTML lands in docs/build/.


CATALOG AND DATA (VBFCatalog)
-----------------------------

VBFCatalog owns the on-disk SQLite catalog (BFA_catalog.sqlite), content-
addressed by LUT SHA-256, with full provenance per row (bib_key, source_repo,
contributor, added_date). Open it with load_catalog(); query with list_functions,
count_functions, find_function; materialise an S-box with load_sbox. Literature
S-boxes load via load_literature_sbox / literature_sbox_names (AES, AES inverse,
and 14 four-bit cipher S-boxes: PRESENT, GIFT, Midori, SKINNY, Piccolo, Serpent
S0..S7), each from a primary specification. APN entries load via
load_apn_function and can be specified directly by exponent. The bibliography
loads via load_bibliography / parse_bibtex from data-raw/bibliography.bib.

The committed catalog has 603 first-class rows: 18 literature S-boxes, 28
declarative APN functions (n in {4,5,6,7,8,9,10}), 530 quadratic-APN CCZ-class
representatives (family APN-rep, from reprs6/7/8 and eightBitAPN), and 27
Simon-Berg CCZ-class representatives (family CCZ-rep-GF4, one per CCZ class of
GF(2^2)-coefficient quadratic APN over GF(2^8)). Cross-source reconciliation
against sboxU, Xanadu, and the APN-Repository is queryable via
reconciliation_by_source, functions_by_source, cross_source_matches,
multi_source_functions; the committed catalog carries roughly 33k reconciliation
rows (n in {6,7,8,9,10}).

Very large function families are held as bulk corpora rather than first-class
rows, so the committed catalog stays small: a bulk_corpora descriptor row plus a
compact side store corpora/<name>.sqlite of quadratic-coefficient encodings, with
each member's LUT reconstructed on demand. Access via list_corpora, corpus_info,
corpus_length, corpus_function, and materialize_corpus_luts (the lazy full-LUT
store). Simon Berg's 196,863-member corpus and the seven literature families of
8-bit quadratic APN (Edel-Pott, Weng-Tan-Gong, Yu-Wang-Li, Taniguchi,
Beierle-Leander, Yu-Perrin, Beierle-Leander-Perrin; 32,916 members, 32,892
distinct up to EA) are stored this way.

Developer-time importers that rebuild BFA_catalog.sqlite live under
packages/VBFCatalog/data-build/ and run in their own standalone environment.
They read the upstream checkouts (paths overridable via BFA_SBOXU_PATH,
BFA_XANADU_PATH, BFA_APN_REPO_PATH, BFA_THESIS_PATH) and are never a run-time
dependency. The importers mutate the committed catalog in place, so do not
include one against the default catalog from a REPL or test; the relevant ones
have a NO_AUTORUN guard for testing against a temporary copy.


PAPER REPRODUCTIONS
-------------------

papers/ holds standalone scripts, each exposing reproduce() and checked against
frozen expected/ outputs in the papers test item. They recompute a published
result with VBF primitives: the boomerang uniformities of Cid et al. (EUROCRYPT
2018), the AES DDT anomaly of Bonnetain-Perrin-Tian (ASIACRYPT 2019), the
ortho-derivative EA-distinction of Canteaut-Couvreur-Perrin (IEEE TIT 2022), the
seven 8-bit quadratic-APN sources listed above, and quadratic_apn_8bit_landscape,
which derives the 32,892 figure by ortho-derivative deduplication of all
re-housed members.


DEVELOPMENT
-----------

Conventions
  - One concept per file, grouped by concept directory. The module docstring at
    the top of a file states the mathematics and the citation; each function has
    its own docstring. Name files and modules by concept and cite the algorithm's
    author or paper in the docstring, not the upstream project a routine came
    from.
  - Every piece is tested. Each test file is a single ReTestItems @testitem named
    <feature>_test.jl under packages/VBF/test/{unit,property}; because items run
    in isolated workers, each declares its own dependencies (using VBF,
    VBFCatalog, ...). Favour high-signal anchors: closed-form counts (for example
    |GL(5,F2)| = 9,999,360, Gaussian binomial subspace counts), round-trips,
    CCZ-invariance under inv(F), and parity between two strategies.
  - Document limitations in the docstring rather than hiding them (for example
    "permutations only", "degenerate partition short-circuits", "linear-code
    equivalence kernel out of scope").

Implementation notes
  - Indexing: the mathematics is 0-based, Julia is 1-based, so an S-box reads
    lut[x+1].
  - Bit-packing conventions differ by structure. FunctionGraph packs
    (x << N) | F(x) (input high, output low); Walsh-zeroes pack (a << N) | b. A
    wrong convention makes round-trips fail silently.
  - Many algorithms are permutations-only (Stennes, the LPP backtracking,
    linear_equivalences). ortho_derivative works for non-bijective quadratic APN,
    but its output may itself be a non-permutation, which then blocks the
    permutation-only downstream routines.
  - For monomial APN, heggebakk's k=4 partition is degenerate ([2^n-1, 1]), so
    the EA outer search would walk all of GL(n,F2); the :ortho_derivative
    strategy is what actually decides EA-equivalence in that case.
  - thickness_spectrum is an EA-invariant, not a CCZ-invariant (verified on GIFT
    versus inv(GIFT)); are_ccz_equivalent uses |Z(F)| and the DDT multiset for
    fast rejection instead.
  - permutation_order must not cap at 2^n; Landau's function exceeds it (PRESENT
    has order 84).

Out of scope
  - A native binary linear-code-equivalence backend (which would enable a
    code-based are_ccz_equivalent; sboxU delegates this to SAGE / Leon's
    algorithm).


REPRODUCIBILITY
---------------

Every public S-box in the catalog carries its provenance (bib_key, source_repo,
contributor, added_date). Independent implementations of EA- and CCZ-equivalence
cross-check the published claims that depend on those relations. The paper
reproductions ship with frozen expected/ outputs and run as part of the test
suite, which CI exercises on every push and pull request against Julia 1.12 and
the latest stable release.


UPSTREAM SOURCES AND CREDITS
----------------------------

VBF reimplements, natively and with no runtime dependency, algorithms and data
from the following projects. Each is credited in the rendered docs' References
page, and at the docstring of the algorithm it backs:

  sboxU                      Léo Perrin, Lukas Stennes, et al. (the linear
                             canonical-representative algorithm and much of the
                             analysis surface). https://github.com/lpp-crypto/sboxU
  ea-equivalence             Marie Heggebakk (the EA-equivalence search; UiB
                             master's thesis, 2022).
                             https://github.com/heggebakk/ea-equivalence
  self_equivalent_apn,       Christof Beierle (self-equivalent and quadratic-APN
  quadratic_apn,             search; trims and extensions).
  Trims-and-extensions       https://github.com/cbe90
  3to1_vbf_equivalence       Ivana Ivković (3-to-1 / triplicate equivalence).
                             https://github.com/telegrin/3to1_vbf_equivalence
  QAM                        Nadiia Ichanska (the QAM construction method).
                             https://github.com/NIchanska/QAM
  APN-Repository             Christian Andersen (APN data and reconciliation).
                             https://github.com/cma029/APN-Repository
  apn-ccz-graph-framework    Xanadu-ins (CCZ-equivalence via graph isomorphism).
                             https://github.com/Xanadu-ins/apn-ccz-graph-framework

Data: the 196,863-member GF(2^2)-coefficient quadratic-APN corpus over GF(2^8)
comes from Simon Berg's master's thesis, "Computational classification and
investigation of cryptographically strong functions" (University of Bergen,
2023).

The primary research papers behind the algorithms are listed on the References
page of the documentation site.


